from ./Output import Output
from ./componentProperties/layout/Display import Display

export anchor Layout:
    description:
        The layout rules Emul follows, in all three outputs, where plain CSS
        leaves room for surprises or where Emul deliberately differs from it.
        Anything not covered here follows CSS.

    noMarginCollapsing:
        Margins never collapse. In CSS, the vertical margins of neighbouring
        blocks can merge into one; in Emul, two blocks with 16px margins
        always have 32px between them, exactly as written.

        The HTML output keeps this true in the browser by laying out every
        block whose children are all blocks as a flex column, which never
        collapses margins. Blocks holding text or inline instances are left
        as normal blocks, where vertical margins don't apply to the inline
        content anyway.

    percentageHeights:
        A percentage height is measured against the parent's height, but
        only when that height is fixed. When the parent's height is `auto`,
        and so depends on its children, a percentage height on a child is
        treated as `auto`, as in CSS. The same applies to minHeight and
        maxHeight.

        The root is the parent of a composition's top-level instances, and
        its height is the canvas height. With a canvas height of `auto`,
        percentage heights on top-level instances are treated as `auto` too.

        Percentage widths are always measured against the parent's width,
        which is always known.

    baselineAlignment:
        `alignItems: baseline` and `alignSelf: baseline` line children up by
        the baseline of their first line of text. A child with no text of its
        own uses the bottom edge of its margin box as its baseline. Text
        inside nested children does not count; only text written directly in
        the child's own body.

    whiteSpace:
        With whiteSpace at its initial value, `normal`, text is handled the
        same way CSS handles it.

        - collapsing
            Any run of spaces or tabs inside quoted text is drawn as a single
            space.
        - joining
            Quoted texts next to each other are joined before collapsing, so a
            space at the end of one and the start of the next become one
            space.
        - trimming
            Spaces at the start and end of a line, once the text has been
            broken into lines, are not drawn.
        - wrapping
            Lines break at the places the Unicode line breaking rules allow,
            such as between words, as in browsers.

        `nowrap` keeps the collapsing but never breaks lines. `pre` and
        `pre-wrap` keep every space as written; `pre-wrap` still wraps.
        `pre-line` collapses spaces and wraps.

    inline:
        @{Display} `inline` is for styling part of a run of text, such as a
        bold word in a sentence. To keep it simple, an inline instance:

        - holds only text and other inline instances
        - is styled only by its typography properties, color, textDecoration
            and textShadow
        - ignores width, height, padding, margin, border and background, which
            are reported as having no effect

        Everything that needs a box, such as a button or a badge inside a
        sentence, is laid out with flex instead.

    imageSize:
        An image instance with no width or height is drawn at the image's
        natural size, which is found as follows.

        - raster images
            PNG, JPEG and WebP files use their size in pixels.
        - svg images
            Use their width and height, or the size of their view box if they
            have none, and `300px` by `150px` if they have neither, as in
            browsers.

        When only one of width or height is set, the other follows from the
        image's own proportions. When both are set, objectFit decides how the
        image fills the box.

    sizingKeywords:
        Sizes are given as lengths, percentages or `auto`. The CSS keywords
        `min-content`, `max-content` and `fit-content` are not part of Emul.

    gradients:
        Gradients follow CSS syntax: `linear-gradient()` with an angle in deg
        or a `to` direction such as `to right`, `radial-gradient()` with a
        shape and position, and colour stops with optional positions in px or
        %.

        Colours between stops are always blended in sRGB, whatever format the
        stops are written in. The HTML output writes `in srgb` into each
        gradient, so browsers blend the same way, and the SVG output converts
        every stop to sRGB, which is how SVG gradients blend.

    textDirection:
        Text is laid out left to right. Right-to-left and mixed-direction
        text, as in Arabic or Hebrew, is out of scope for now.

    accuracy:
        These rules make the three outputs match closely, but not to the
        pixel; see @{Output}.
